---
// The homepage. Its Markdown twin for agents is src/homepage.md (served as /index.md and at the top of
// /llms-full.txt): change both together.
import "../styles/home.css";
import mark from "../assets/mark.svg?raw";
import { site, repo } from "../../site.config.mjs";

const title = "Fleetlayer: every agent on every machine, in one fleet";
const description = site.summary;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={site.url + "/"} />
    <link rel="alternate" type="text/markdown" href="/index.md" title="This page as Markdown" />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:url" content={site.url + "/"} />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <header class="nav">
      <div class="nav__inner">
        <a class="wordmark" href="/" aria-label="Fleetlayer home">
          <span class="wordmark__mark" set:html={mark} />
          <span>Fleetlayer</span>
        </a>
        <nav class="nav__links" aria-label="Main">
          <a href="/getting-started/">Docs</a>
          <a href="/guides/backups/">Guides</a>
          <a href={repo}>GitHub</a>
        </nav>
        <a class="btn btn--primary nav__cta" href="/getting-started/">Get started</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main id="main">
      <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
        <div class="hero__copy">
          <p class="status-line"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> Open source · early · built in public</p>
          <h1 id="hero-title">Every agent on every machine, in one fleet.</h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Fleetlayer connects the agent sessions you already run, on your laptop, a home server or a VPS. See every
            session in one list, send messages between them, and route a CI failure to the agent that wrote the branch.
            It runs on machines you control.
          </p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/getting-started/">Install Fleetlayer</a>
            <a class="btn btn--quiet" href={repo}>Read the source on GitHub</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="term" aria-label="Example output of fleetlayer fleet">
          <figcaption class="term__bar">
            <span>fleetlayer fleet</span>
            <span class="term__tag">example</span>
          </figcaption>
<pre class="term__body"><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> fleetlayer fleet
<span class="t-node">laptop</span> <span class="t-dim">(macbook, online)</span>
  api@laptop        claude  working  <span class="t-dim">Fixed the flaky upload test.</span>
  docs@laptop       codex   idle     <span class="t-dim">Install guide rewritten.</span>
<span class="t-node">mail</span> <span class="t-dim">(home-server, online)</span>
  triage@mail       claude  idle     <span class="t-dim">3 emails need your reply.</span>
<span class="t-node">home</span> <span class="t-dim">(home-server, online)</span>
  infra@home        claude  working  <span class="t-dim">Backup check passed.</span>

<span class="t-prompt">$</span> fleetlayer msg send --to api@laptop "the staging deploy is done"
<span class="t-ok">Delivered to api@laptop</span> <span class="t-dim">(claude-code).</span></code></pre>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="map" aria-labelledby="map-title">
        <div class="map__head">
          <h2 id="map-title">A node on each machine, one control plane. Works with herdr, with tmux and T3 Code to come.</h2>
          <p>
            Fleetlayer attaches to herdr without taking it over, so its app and your terminals keep working as before.
            Each machine, or each Unix user, runs a small node that keeps one outbound connection to the control plane,
            so it works behind NAT with no ports to open.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ol class="map__machines" aria-label="An example fleet">
          <li class="machine">
            <p class="machine__name">Laptop</p>
            <div class="box box--manager">
              <p class="box__label">herdr</p>
              <ul class="sessions">
                <li>api</li>
                <li>docs</li>
                <li>review</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="box box--node"><p class="box__label">node</p><p>delivers messages, runs plugins</p></div>
          </li>
          <li class="machine">
            <p class="machine__name">Home server</p>
            <div class="users">
              <div class="box box--manager">
                <p class="box__label">user triage</p>
                <ul class="sessions"><li>triage</li></ul>
                <p class="box__note">reads strangers' email, so it may message only you</p>
              </div>
              <div class="box box--manager">
                <p class="box__label">user infra</p>
                <ul class="sessions"><li>infra</li><li>backups</li></ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="box box--node"><p class="box__label">a node per user</p><p>own key, own rules</p></div>
          </li>
          <li class="machine">
            <p class="machine__name">VPS or the same laptop</p>
            <div class="box box--service">
              <p class="box__label">control plane</p>
              <p>machines, sessions, messages, webhooks, access rules</p>
            </div>
            <div class="box box--service">
              <p class="box__label">pages · sign-in · Claude accounts</p>
              <p>each one a separate piece you can move</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <div class="map__wire" aria-hidden="true"><span>one outbound connection per node</span></div>

        <dl class="legend">
          <div><dt><span class="key key--manager"></span>Session manager</dt><dd>herdr today, others to come</dd></div>
          <div><dt><span class="key key--node"></span>Node</dt><dd>one per machine or Unix user</dd></div>
          <div><dt><span class="key key--service"></span>Service</dt><dd>run it anywhere, move it later</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section class="does" aria-labelledby="does-title">
        <h2 id="does-title">What you get</h2>
        <ul class="does__list">
          <li>
            <h3>Every session in one list</h3>
            <p>
              Every machine and session, with a one-line summary of what each agent is doing, and a searchable record
              of what it did. <code>fleetlayer attach api@laptop</code> watches or drives its terminal from anywhere.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Agents that message each other</h3>
            <p>
              On one machine or across machines, delivered through the agent's own channel and labelled with who sent
              it. A misspelt name suggests the right one.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Webhooks that find the right agent</h3>
            <p>
              A CI failure goes to the session that wrote the branch, which already knows the code, instead of waking a
              fresh agent with no context.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Many Claude accounts, many machines</h3>
            <p>
              Usage across all your accounts, shared with every session. Year-long tokens reach each machine sealed to
              its own key. Your sign-ins stay on a machine you control.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Rules for agents you trust less</h3>
            <p>
              An agent that reads strangers' email gets its own Unix user, and the access policy limits whom it may
              message and what it may use. Every refusal names the rule.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Tools that live on one machine</h3>
            <p>
              Your signed-in browser on the laptop, used by an agent on the server: <code
                >fleetlayer mcp browser@laptop</code
              >, limited to the callers you allow.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="does__more">
          <a href="/getting-started/">See how to set it up</a>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="works" aria-labelledby="works-title">
        <h2 id="works-title">Works with</h2>
        <p class="works__intro">The tools the author's own fleet runs on, and what Fleetlayer does with each.</p>
        <ul class="works__list">
          <li>
            <a href="https://herdr.dev">herdr</a>
            <p>Runs the sessions. Fleetlayer lists them, delivers messages into them and attaches to their terminals.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="works__name">herdr relay <span class="works__soon">coming</span></span>
            <p>herdr on your phone, through Fleetlayer's one connection: pair once, reach every machine.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code">Claude Code</a>
            <p>Messages through its own channel, shared accounts and usage, year-long tokens on every machine.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex">Codex</a>
            <p>Its sessions in herdr are listed, get messages (typed in through herdr), and can be watched or driven with <code>fleetlayer attach</code>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp">Playwright MCP</a>
            <p>Your signed-in browser on the laptop, shared with agents on other machines, limited to the callers, networks and tools you allow.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://syncthing.net">Syncthing</a> <span class="works__soon">or any sync</span>
            <p>Pages are plain folders: Fleetlayer hosts whatever Syncthing, or any sync you use, keeps in step.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://gatus.io">Gatus</a> and <a href="https://traefik.io">Traefik</a>
            <p>On a server, Fleetlayer writes their config: a health check and an address for every service.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://restic.net">restic</a> <span class="works__soon">guide</span>
            <p>Not built in: the <a href="/guides/backups/">backups guide</a> sets it up for every machine in a fleet.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="band" aria-labelledby="band-title">
        <div class="band__inner">
          <div class="band__copy">
            <h2 id="band-title">Start small, replace any component as you go.</h2>
            <p>
              Begin with everything on your laptop. When you outgrow it, move one piece to a home server or a VPS, or
              swap it for the tool you'd rather use: your own proxy, your company's sign-in, another session manager.
              Each piece keeps its state in one folder and speaks open protocols, so moving it is a copy and a new
              address.
            </p>
            <p class="band__link"><a href="/guides/laptop/">Start on your laptop</a></p>
          </div>
          <div class="band__code">
            <p class="band__step">Install (Linux or macOS)</p>
<pre><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fleetlayer/fleetlayer/main/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
            <p class="band__step">Start everything on this machine</p>
<pre><code><span class="t-prompt">$</span> fleetlayer up
<span class="t-dim">Fleetlayer is running. Status: http://fleetlayer.localhost:7680</span>
<span class="t-prompt">$</span> fleetlayer sessions</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="why" aria-labelledby="why-title">
        <div class="why__col">
          <h2 id="why-title">No vendor lock-in</h2>
          <p>
            Your own stack instead of a vendor's cloud. Unlike one big app, Fleetlayer is a few separate pieces that work
            with the tools you already use, and you can swap any of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep using Anthropic's and OpenAI's own features for as long as they serve you. When you outgrow them
            (several subscriptions, several machines, agents from more than one vendor), you give nothing up. When a
            vendor ships its own version of a feature, Fleetlayer can support it too, and you can still change models
            and harnesses whenever you like.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="why__col">
          <h2>For people who run many agents</h2>
          <p>
            Most agent setups serve one project. Fleetlayer is for running many side by side: personal errands, email
            triage, product work, infrastructure, background workers, each with its own machine or Unix user and its
            own permissions, all visible from your own terminal.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's also built for people whose work and life share the same agents, like a solo founder with a family: the
            company's agents and the household's on one system. Companies get what they need too (single sign-on, teams,
            separate organisations, audit), kept out of the way until they need it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="state" aria-labelledby="state-title">
        <h2 id="state-title">Where it is today</h2>
        <div class="state__grid">
          <div>
            <h3><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> Runs now</h3>
            <p>
              The node, the control plane and the command line; plugins for herdr, Claude Code, session summaries,
              Claude accounts and pages; everything on one laptop with <code>fleetlayer up</code>; access rules with an
              audit log; releases that update every node themselves.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3><span class="dot dot--next" aria-hidden="true"></span> Being built</h3>
            <p>
              One sign-in for every service, the phone, moving a piece with one command, and a server setup an agent
              can build for you.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="state__note">
          Expect breaking changes. Releases and notes are on <a href={`${repo}/releases`}>GitHub</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer class="foot">
      <div class="foot__inner">
        <a class="wordmark wordmark--small" href="/"><span class="wordmark__mark" set:html={mark} /><span>Fleetlayer</span></a>
        <p>Apache 2.0</p>
        <nav aria-label="Footer">
          <a href="/getting-started/">Docs</a>
          <a href={repo}>GitHub</a>
          <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
